{% set local_problem %}
<p>We've lived and worked in Hartselle long enough to know exactly how fast gutters fill up around here. The established <a href="/recent-projects/">neighborhoods downtown</a> have mature trees dropping leaves by the truckload every fall. Properties outside town often have pine coverage shedding needles constantly. Even newer developments have enough tree coverage to keep gutters filling.</p>

<p>When gutters clog, problems start quickly. Overflowing water runs down your siding, pools around your foundation, and eventually finds its way into places it shouldn't. Morgan County's clay-heavy soil means pooled water often stays pooled for days, creating conditions for foundation damage and basement moisture.</p>

<p>Many Hartselle homeowners try handling gutter cleaning themselves. It's time-consuming and potentially dangerous work. Climbing extension ladders, reaching across rooflines, dealing with decomposed debris — not a pleasant way to spend a Saturday. And for homes with steep roofs or two stories, DIY cleaning can be genuinely risky.</p>

<p><strong>The math:</strong> Foundation repair in Morgan County runs $10,000-30,000 depending on severity. Annual gutter cleaning runs $250-400 total. We've watched neighbors skip cleanings to save money, then spend five figures fixing foundation cracks. Don't learn this lesson the expensive way.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Our gutter cleaning is comprehensive — not the quick blow-and-go some services offer.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Hand removal.</strong> We pull all debris from gutter troughs by hand — leaves, pine needles, roof grit, decomposed organic matter. We don't blow debris onto your lawn or push it toward downspouts (a common shortcut that causes clogs downstream). We bag it and haul it away.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets flushed with water until it runs clear. This is critical. Downspout clogs are the #1 thing homeowners miss during DIY cleaning. Debris backs up in the downspout, water can't exit, and gutters overflow even though they look clean from above.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check for: loose or missing hangers, seam separations, small leaks, rust spots, and fascia showing early damage. We document problems and let you know what we find — no scare tactics, just honest assessment.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Cleanup.</strong> When we're done, your property looks better than when we arrived. No debris on the lawn, no mess in the landscaping.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> Tell you everything is "fine" when it's not. We see problems constantly that previous cleaners ignored. You'll get an honest picture of what's happening up there.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>This is our hometown. We're not a franchise from somewhere else — we're the local company that's been serving Hartselle for over two decades. Our mailing address is P.O. Box 103, right here in town.</p>

<p>When you call Blue River Gutters, you're calling neighbors who care about this community. We show up when we say we will, do the job right, and charge a fair price. Pretty simple, really — it's how things should work when you're dealing with people you might see at the grocery store.</p>

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you hire someone for gutter cleaning and they don't flush your downspouts, you're not getting complete service. Debris compacts into partial clogs that restrict flow — and you can't see them from above. Ask specifically. Some companies skip this step because it takes extra time. We don't.</p>

<p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> For most <a href="/recent-projects/">Hartselle homes</a>, schedule cleanings in late November (after leaves drop) and late April (after spring pollen/seeds). If you have significant pine coverage, add one in August. Three cleanings a year keeps everything flowing. Or consider gutter guards if you're tired of the ongoing expense.</p>
